# .NET Framework → .NET 8 Migration: `EventProcessor`

## Critical Pitfalls in the Original Code

| Issue | Why it breaks on .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed entirely |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Still exists but won't interrupt `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` — only sleeps/joins |
| `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` | Race condition: multiple `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`/`Reset` lose signals, dropping items |
| Raw `Thread` + `lock` | Unnecessary complexity — modern primitives handle this natively |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _runTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _runTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
        {
            Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Channel closed.");
    }

    public async ValueTask StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();         // signal no more items
        _cts?.Cancel();                     // interrupt if blocked waiting

        if (_runTask is not null)
        {
            using var timeoutCts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
            try
            {
                await _runTask.WaitAsync(timeoutCts.Token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) { /* timed out or cancelled — acceptable */ }
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item)
    {
        // your processing logic
    }
}
```

## Key Changes Explained

**1. `Channel<T>` replaces `Queue` + `ManualResetEvent` + `lock`**
- Thread-safe, backpressure-aware, no lost signals
- `ReadAllAsync` yields items as they arrive — no manual wait/reset

**2. `CancellationToken` replaces `Thread.Abort/Interrupt`**
- Cooperative cancellation is the only supported model in .NET 8
- `ReadAllAsync(ct)` throws `OperationCanceledException` on cancel

**3. `Task.Run` replaces `new Thread`**
- Uses the thread pool; avoids dedicated thread overhead
- Returns a `Task` you can `await` for clean shutdown

**4. `IAsyncDisposable` for resource cleanup**
- Ensures the background loop terminates even if `StopAsync` isn't called

## If You Need Synchronous Stop (legacy API surface)

```csharp
public void Stop()
{
    _channel.Writer.Complete();
    _cts?.Cancel();
    _runTask?.Wait(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));  // blocking equivalent
}
```

## If You Need a Dedicated Thread (long-running CPU work)

```csharp
_runTask = Task.Factory.StartNew(
    () => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token),
    _cts.Token,
    TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning,
    TaskScheduler.Default
).Unwrap();
```

This avoids starving the thread pool when `Handle()` is CPU-intensive.
